WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.600
Unrestrained by typical TV standards, Netflix
is able to produce and distribute a neary

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.639
unlimited amount of original content at a
spectacular rate, including an array of high-quality

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.849
offerings such as Stranger Things and Unbreakable
Kimmy Schmidt.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:16.460
But that doesn’t mean it hasn’t put out
a few duds over the years.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.980
Since it can be pretty difficult to keep up
with everything Netflix puts out these days,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.099
we've rounded up some of the best stuff the
company has introduced since the beginning

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.660
of 2018... along with a few you’re really
better off avoiding.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.940
Best : Lost in Space

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.120
TV sci-fi was very much in its infancy when
the original Lost in Space debuted in the

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.039
1960s, so the show never looked particularly
good, despite its ambitious premise.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.520
“Yes, spare us your ridiculous remarks,
you insensitive idiot.”

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:50.070
Netflix redeemed the Lost in Space name by
rebooting it as an epic drama, full of multiplex-level

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.040
special effects.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.100
The characters have been refined too, driven
by the talents of a top-notch cast.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.690
In this reboot, Molly Parker takes the lead
as an aerospace engineer attempting to lead

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.860
her family to a colony in Alpha Centauri.

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:06.800
The villain has also been gender-flipped,
with the iconic Dr. Smith now played to a

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.400
hilt by indie movie queen Parker Posey.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.940
“I go where you go… not that I don’t
trust you.”

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.900
Worst : The Cloverfield Paradox

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.360
The third installment in the Cloverfield anthology
finds a team of scientists, played by Daniel

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.770
Bruhl, Elizabeth Debicki, and Chris O'Dowd,
stranded on a remote space station, attempting

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.120
to tap a new source of energy.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.890
Instead, they mishandle a particle accelerator,
wind up in an alternate dimension, and send

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.320
a familiar monster hurtling down to Earth.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.580
Basically, think Alien, only more convoluted,
and led by a plot too thin to justify its

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.700
many twists and turns.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.200
Best : Wild Wild Country

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:01.140
This six-part documentary series is an intricate
and almost unbelievable story of crime, corruption,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.160
exploitation, spirituality, and human darkness.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:09.160
It tells the story of a commune built by followers
of the Indian spiritual guru Bhagwan Shree

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:15.160
Rajneesh in 1981, and the grim events which
took place after things began to spiral out

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:15.780
of control.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.740
The series tells this bizarre and frightening
tale in a slow and precise manner, with plenty

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:26.240
of archival footage and interviews with former
followers of Rajneesh, many of whom were close-knit

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.130
members of a group known for its mass stockpiling
of weapons, heavy reliance on arson, and penchant

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.520
for poisoning salad bars.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:34.880
It's not to be missed.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:36.760
Worst : Mute

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.770
Mute had everything going for it.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:44.640
The cast included a host of stars like Justin
Theroux, Paul Rudd and Alexander Skarsgard.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.960
It was co-written and directed by Duncan Jones,
the mind behind modern-day sci-fi classics

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.480
like Moon and Source Code.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.720
Even the premise, a mute bartender searching
for his missing love in a futuristic Berlin,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.100
seemed like it was full of potential.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.580
But when Mute finally hit Netflix in 2018,
fans were left disappointed.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.680
Rosie Fletcher of Digital Spy summarized the
movie best, saying:

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.180
"The whole film is populated by people you
don't believe or care at all about, behaving

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.430
in ways that don't make sense against a cheap-looking
sci-fi-by-numbers backdrop."

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.960
“Daddy always did say, ‘Girls are nothing
but trouble’.”

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.580
Best : A Futile and Stupid Gesture

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.510
On its surface, it's the story of the beginning
of the National Lampoon, the premiere humor

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:36.090
magazine of the 1970s and early '80s, but
A Futile and Stupid Gesture is also the life

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.220
story of its co-founder Doug Kenney.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:40.320
“Tempting.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.540
Glad you finally saw the light.”

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.220
The Lampoon was an incredibly influential
brand, staffed by personnel who went on to

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.260
produce a range of iconic comedies, including
Caddyshack and Animal House.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.400
The cast is bursting with comedy stars, all
paying tribute to their heroes.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.620
Joel McHale portrays his Community co-star
Chevy Chase, while Seth Green pops up as Christopher

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.120
Guest.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.260
Will Forte is a revelation here too, turning
in an Oscar-worthy performance as Kenney,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.240
a gifted comic genius who suffers at the whims
of his own demons, even as he goes on to change

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:11.740
the world.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.239
"Yeah, so, these actors don’t look exactly
like the real people, but c’mon.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.160
Do you think I looked like Will Forte when
I was 27?

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.280
Do you think Will Forte is 27?”

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.580
Worst : Game Over, Man!

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.150
If you weren’t a fan of the long-running
Comedy Central sitcom Workaholics, you’re

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.100
probably not going to find much to love here.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.880
Game Over, Man! takes everything that was
thrown into that show and dials it up to eleven.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.220
Jokes about genitalia?

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.020
Tons of them.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.560
Romanticized '80s action-movie violence?

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:40.500
Endless.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.760
Irreverent, profanity-laden banter between
the three leads?

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:44.800
You betcha.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.610
The movie’s thin plot is essentially a dull
rehash of Die Hard, which isn’t enough to

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.750
mask the fact that Anders Holm, Blake Anderson
and Adam Devine are still portraying the exact

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.690
same character types they played on Workaholics.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.840
Great if you loved that show; not so great
if you didn’t.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.180
Best : Altered Carbon

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:08.260
Based on the 2002 novel by Richard K. Morgan,
Altered Carbon is set in a far-flung future

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.560
in which scientists can upload a person's
memories onto an implantable disk.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:17.820
It’s essentially a highly-stylized crime
story, filled to the brim with body-switching,

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.190
sci-fi tech and murder.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.250
“The partner’s frequently murdered.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.160
Alas, maybe being an AI makes that less likely.”

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:26.520
“No.”

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.520
Although both tremendously violent and sexually
charged, Altered Carbon also explores identity

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.200
on a philosophical level that sci-fi hasn't
matched since The Matrix.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.520
Worst : The Week Of

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:44.730
The Week Of places the now-50-year-old Adam
Sandler in the "doofus dad" phase of his career,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:50.110
while also giving us a new Sandler to go along
with Silly Sandler and Serious Actor Sandler:

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:50.640
Boring Sandler.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.960
“Isaac likes Jeremy Lin?

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.500
And he’s aware Jeremy Lin no longer plays
for the New York Knicks?”

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:57.700
“It’s a very nice shirt.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.260
Seven dollars at Modell’s.”

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.480
Seriously, very little happens in this movie.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.220
It takes place during the week of his daughter's
wedding to the son of a surgeon, played, probably

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.820
unsurprisingly, by Chris Rock.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:13.220
Sandler gets into some slapstick, messes up
preparations for the big day and cringes half-convincingly

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.720
at the price tag the whole way through.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.680
To his credit, the title is appropriate: The
Week Of really does feel like it takes that

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.240
long to watch.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.660
Best : Queer Eye

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:27.580
In the 2000s, Queer Eye for the Straight Guy
launched the "metrosexual" movement.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.800
Five homosexual guys, stereotypically renowned
for their excellent tastes in fashion, interior

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:39.070
design, culture, grooming, and cuisine, provided
makeovers for slovenly heterosexual men, stereotypically

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.320
renowned to be woefully incompetent in matters
of taste.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.280
"I love a good designer meat."

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.000
While the original Queer Eye was plenty of
fun, Netflix’s reboot is something altogether

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:50.540
more vital.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.600
During a time of political and cultural division,
Queer Eye makes for a series of moving tales

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.220
of personal connection, acceptance and healing.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:03.030
And the fact that the regular cast are so
downright charismatic and inspiring doesn’t

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.030
exactly hurt things, either.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.140
“We’ve all go to come together in a way
where we can understand each other.”

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.040
“Guys!

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:08.860
Where are we going?”

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:09.360
“Georgia?”

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:10.480
“Let’s do it!”

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.560
“Yay!”

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.160
Worst : A Little Help with Carol Burnett

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.780
In an unfortunate turn, Carol Burnett's first
regular TV series in decades has turned out

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:24.660
to be a forced, awkward mashup of Dr. Phil
and Kids Say the Darndest Things.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:30.020
In each episode, Burnett, along with criminally
misused co-host Russell Peters, takes questions

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.160
about life and love and all things related
from a studio audience.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:38.620
Then Burnett, usually joined by a celebrity
guest, attempts to scramble an answer together

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.310
before throwing the query over to a panel
of cute and precocious kids who quite clearly

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:44.800
have no idea what they're talking about.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:52.140
"I'm not trying to be rude, but maybe you
just, like, don't have a friend."

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.280
It’s not exactly riveting stuff.

